course: dictionary
lesson: 1730
title: Civilisation: a society with cities and laws
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A civilisation is a large, organised society. It has cities, a government, writing, laws and a shared culture. «Chinese civilisation grew along two great rivers, the Yellow River and the Yangtze.» The clues are the big rivers and the word grew, which tell you it was a large group of people living together in a planned way.
explain: The word also has other meanings. Historians use it for the whole way of life of an advanced society, as in the history of civilisation. Informally, people say «back to civilisation» after camping, meaning back to towns with comforts such as beds and showers. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check which one fits.
explain: Some civilisations are famous: ancient Egypt, the Indus Valley, the Maya and the Incas. «Egypt is mostly desert, yet one of the greatest civilisations grew there, all thanks to one river, the Nile.» The word yet is a clue: it shows a surprise, because a civilisation grew where most of the land was dry.
explain: Many early civilisations began by rivers. Rivers could bring disasters too. «The Yellow River's floods brought rich soil but also terrible disasters.» The word but tells you the sentence has two sides: a good thing and a bad thing.
fact: A civilisation is a large, organised society with cities, government, writing, laws and culture.
fact: Examples of early civilisations include Sumer, ancient Egypt, the Indus Valley, the Shang dynasty in China, ancient Greece, the Maya, the Aztecs and the Incas.
fact: Many early civilisations began by rivers, where the soil was rich.
fact: «Five thousand years» is a traditional phrase that includes legendary times, so it is not an exact count.
fact: Historians now avoid calling some peoples more civilised than others.
exercise: «Chinese civilisation grew along two great rivers, the Yellow River and the Yangtze.» What does civilisation mean here? | A type of rain that falls on rivers | A long history book | *A large, organised society with cities, government and writing | A small group of friends who go camping together
exercise: Which of these is an example of an early civilisation? | A camping site in the hills | A modern shopping centre | *Ancient Egypt, beside the Nile | A football club
exercise: Dad says, «We are back to civilisation now the camping trip is over.» What does civilisation mean here? | Deep forests with no paths | *Towns with comforts such as beds and showers | A kind of tent | A long history book
test: Why did many early civilisations begin by rivers? | *Rivers gave water and rich soil for farming | Rivers stopped people from living nearby | Rivers were only used for swimming | Rivers were always dry and empty
test: Which word in «The Yellow River's floods brought rich soil but also terrible disasters» shows the sentence has two sides? | Yellow | floods | *but | rich
exam: What do historians now avoid doing? | Writing about ancient Egypt | *Calling some peoples more civilised than others | Using the word civilisation at all | Studying the Nile
